@meta
id: 15-10
title: Off the line and back
chapter: 15
type: lesson
skills: global-normal
par: 35
minutes: 6
vscode: find @deprecated, select each method by dragging, Delete, three times
open: order.provider.ts

@brief
Three methods in `OrderProvider` carry a `@deprecated` doc comment, and
the last caller of each was removed last sprint. Delete all three, each
with its comment line, and leave exactly one blank line between the
methods that remain.

The pattern only matches the comment line. The method is the three
lines under it. So the `normal` sequence has to do something that
`d` on the marked line alone cannot: reach off the line and take the
whole block.

You already own the right tool. A doc comment sitting directly on top
of its method, with blank lines above and below, is a paragraph.

@keys
| `:g/pattern/normal keys` | replay `keys` on every matching line |
| `dap` | delete a paragraph and the blank line after it |

@hints
Do not think about the method's lines. Think about what shape the
comment and the method make together, and which text object names it.
--
`dap` from the comment line takes the comment, the method under it and
the blank line that follows. That is exactly the three-method cleanup,
once per match.
--
`:g/@deprecated/normal dap` and Enter.

@solution
    :g/@deprecated/normal dap<Enter>  :wq<Enter>

Thirty keystrokes to remove fifteen lines in three places.

This is the step where `:g//normal` stops being a way to save keystrokes
and becomes a different kind of tool. The marked line is only a starting
point. From there the keys can go anywhere: `j` to the line below, `}`
to the end of the block, `dap` around the whole thing, `%` to the
matching brace. The pattern finds the landmark; the keys do the work
around it.

One thing to check before you lean on `dap`: a paragraph ends at a blank
line, so a method with a blank line inside its body is two paragraphs,
and `dap` would take only the first. These three are one-liners. For
the longer kind you would reach for `j` and `%` or `d}` instead; the
principle is the same, only the text object changes.

There is a subtlety worth knowing. `:g` marks every match before it runs
anything. If a command deletes a line that was marked later in the file,
that mark disappears with the line and the command is simply not run
there. So overlapping edits do not double up, and you do not need to
worry about a `dap` swallowing the next match.

If you tried `dd` on the comment and then `dap` on the method you were
working too hard. Look for the text object that names the whole shape
first; there usually is one.

@start order.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';
import { PaymentService } from '../../infrastructure/services/payment.service';

@injectable()
export class OrderProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(OrderRepository) protected orderRepository: OrderRepository,
    @inject(PaymentService) protected paymentService: PaymentService,
  ) {}

  public async findOne(id: string) {
    const order = await this.orderRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });

    if (!order) {
      throw exception('ORDER_NOT_FOUND');
    }

    return order;
  }

  /** @deprecated use findOne and check the status yourself */
  public async findPending(id: string) {
    return this.orderRepository.findOne({ where: { id, status: 'PENDING' } });
  }

  public async cancel(id: string) {
    const order = await this.findOne(id);

    if (order.status === 'SHIPPED') {
      throw exception('ORDER_ALREADY_SHIPPED');
    }

    await this.paymentService.refund(order.id);

    return this.orderRepository.update({
      where: { id },
      values: { status: 'CANCELLED' },
    });
  }

  /** @deprecated the payment service refunds directly now */
  public async legacyRefund(id: string) {
    return this.paymentService.refundLegacy(id);
  }

  /** @deprecated replaced by the shipping consumer */
  public async markShipped(id: string) {
    return this.orderRepository.update({ where: { id }, values: { status: 'SHIPPED' } });
  }

  public async findByCustomer(customerId: string) {
    return this.orderRepository.findByCustomer({ customerId });
  }
}

@expected order.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';
import { PaymentService } from '../../infrastructure/services/payment.service';

@injectable()
export class OrderProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(OrderRepository) protected orderRepository: OrderRepository,
    @inject(PaymentService) protected paymentService: PaymentService,
  ) {}

  public async findOne(id: string) {
    const order = await this.orderRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });

    if (!order) {
      throw exception('ORDER_NOT_FOUND');
    }

    return order;
  }

  public async cancel(id: string) {
    const order = await this.findOne(id);

    if (order.status === 'SHIPPED') {
      throw exception('ORDER_ALREADY_SHIPPED');
    }

    await this.paymentService.refund(order.id);

    return this.orderRepository.update({
      where: { id },
      values: { status: 'CANCELLED' },
    });
  }

  public async findByCustomer(customerId: string) {
    return this.orderRepository.findByCustomer({ customerId });
  }
}
